Now I have all the pieces. Here's the full report.

## 1. `TerminalStatusBar.swift` — failure-banner / Investigate popover region

File: `/Users/joelmoss/dev/workroom/macapp/WorkroomApp/Views/TerminalStatusBar.swift`

State it has in hand for this view (lines 12–38):
```swift
struct TerminalStatusBar: View {
  let target: TerminalTarget
  let tabID: TerminalTab.ID
  /// The pane's terminal, or nil for a non-terminal (diff / file / changeset) pane — which shows no
  /// cwd, run state or diagnosis, only the path (where there is one) and the branch.
  let state: TerminalState?
  ...
  @ObservedObject var sessions: TerminalSessions
  @EnvironmentObject var store: AppStore
  @EnvironmentObject var agentManager: TerminalAgentManager
  @EnvironmentObject var resumeCoordinator: AgentResumeCoordinator
  @State private var showingDiagnosis = false
  ...
  /// This pane's live cwd (observed state first, then the surface's last-known); nil for a diff pane.
  private var cwd: String? { state.flatMap { $0.cwd ?? $0.view.lastKnownCwd } }
  ...
  private var diagnosis: AgentBannerState? { state == nil ? nil : agentManager.banners[tabID] }
```

The full popover region (lines 297–338):
```swift
  private func diagnosisSegment(_ bannerState: AgentBannerState) -> some View {
    let model = AgentBannerViewModel(state: bannerState)
    let tint = diagnosisTint(model.style)
    return Button {
      showingDiagnosis.toggle()
    } label: {
      HStack(spacing: 4) {
        diagnosisIcon(model.style)
        Text(model.headline).truncationMode(.tail).frame(maxWidth: 220, alignment: .leading)
      }
      // A diagnosis means a command FAILED — colour it red on any tab (not only the run tab's run
      // state), so a failure reads red everywhere.
      .foregroundStyle(tint)
    }
    .buttonStyle(.plain)
    .help("Show the agent's diagnosis")
    .accessibilityLabel("Diagnosis: \(model.headline)")
    .accessibilityIdentifier("terminal.statusBar.diagnosis")
    .popover(isPresented: $showingDiagnosis, arrowEdge: .bottom) {
      TerminalAgentBanner(
        state: bannerState,
        onDiagnose: { agentManager.diagnose(tab: tabID, target: target.id) },
        onInsertFix: { fix in
          state?.view.sendText(fix)
          showingDiagnosis = false
        },
        onInvestigate: {
          let cwd = state?.view.lastKnownCwd ?? target.path
          // Seed the interactive agent with the problem so it can start investigating immediately.
          let command = AgentPrompt.investigateCommandLine(for: bannerState)
          _ = sessions.addRunTab(for: target, command: command, cwd: cwd)
          agentManager.dismiss(tab: tabID)
          showingDiagnosis = false
        },
        onDismiss: {
          agentManager.dismiss(tab: tabID)
          showingDiagnosis = false
        }
      )
      .frame(width: 380)
    }
  }
```

The `onInvestigate` closure (lines 323–330) captures:
- `bannerState` — the function's own parameter (an `AgentBannerState`, taken from `diagnosis` at the call site `if let diagnosis { diagnosisSegment(diagnosis) }`, line 59), used to build the seeded prompt via `AgentPrompt.investigateCommandLine(for: bannerState)`.
- `target` — the struct's `let target: TerminalTarget` property, used both for `target.path` (cwd fallback) and as the `for:` argument to `addRunTab`.
- `sessions` — the struct's `@ObservedObject var sessions: TerminalSessions`, called directly as `sessions.addRunTab(...)`.
- `tabID` — the struct's `let tabID: TerminalTab.ID`, used for `agentManager.dismiss(tab: tabID)`.
- `showingDiagnosis` — the local `@State` toggled off after firing.

`cwd` in this closure is derived as `state?.view.lastKnownCwd ?? target.path` — i.e. from the pane's own `TerminalState?` (`state.view.lastKnownCwd`), falling back to `target.path` (the workroom/target root) if `state` or `lastKnownCwd` is nil. Note this is a *local* `let cwd` shadowing the struct's private computed `cwd` property (which additionally checks `state.cwd` first, see line 34) — the closure does not use that property, it recomputes directly from `state?.view.lastKnownCwd`.

## 2. `TerminalTabStrip.swift` — equivalent popover + Investigate closure

File: `/Users/joelmoss/dev/workroom/macapp/WorkroomApp/Views/TerminalTabStrip.swift`

State available to `TerminalTabStrip` (lines 17–44):
```swift
struct TerminalTabStrip: View {
  let tabs: [TerminalTab]
  let activeID: TerminalTab.ID?
  let target: TerminalTarget
  @ObservedObject var sessions: TerminalSessions
  @EnvironmentObject var store: AppStore
  @EnvironmentObject var notifications: NotificationCenterStore
  @EnvironmentObject var agentManager: TerminalAgentManager
  @Environment(\.accessibilityReduceMotion) private var reduceMotion
  /// The tab whose ✦ agent popover is open.
  @State private var agentPopoverTab: TerminalTab.ID?
  ...
```

It does **not** have an `AgentBannerState` in hand as a stored/passed value — there is no `state`/`bannerState` property analogous to `TerminalStatusBar`'s `state: TerminalState?`. It only has `agentManager` (EnvironmentObject) and looks the banner up by tab id at render/action time via `agentManager.banners[tab.id]`.

The popover attachment site (lines 159–165, inside `chipRun`):
```swift
        .popover(
          isPresented: Binding(
            get: { agentPopoverTab == tab.id },
            set: { if !$0 { agentPopoverTab = nil } })
        ) {
          agentPopover(for: tab)
        }
```

The full popover builder function (lines 427–455):
```swift
  /// The ✦ agent popover for a tab (inline presentation): the diagnosis + its actions, off the grid.
  @ViewBuilder private func agentPopover(for tab: TerminalTab) -> some View {
    if let state = agentManager.banners[tab.id] {
      TerminalAgentBanner(
        state: state,
        onDiagnose: { agentManager.diagnose(tab: tab.id, target: target.id) },
        onInsertFix: { fix in
          if case .terminal(let s) = tab.content { s.view.sendText(fix) }
          agentPopoverTab = nil
        },
        onInvestigate: {
          let cwd: String
          if case .terminal(let s) = tab.content {
            cwd = s.view.lastKnownCwd ?? target.path
          } else {
            cwd = target.path
          }
          _ = store.terminals.addRunTab(for: target, command: "claude", cwd: cwd)
          agentManager.dismiss(tab: tab.id)
          agentPopoverTab = nil
        },
        onDismiss: {
          agentManager.dismiss(tab: tab.id)
          agentPopoverTab = nil
        }
      )
      .frame(width: 360)
    }
  }
```

The `onInvestigate` closure (lines 437–446) captures:
- `tab` — the function's `for tab: TerminalTab` parameter, pattern-matched via `tab.content` (`enum TabContent { case terminal(TerminalState); case diff(...); case file(...); case changeset(...) }`, `TerminalSessions.swift:101-102`) to reach the `TerminalState` (`s`) only when the tab is a terminal.
- `target` — struct's `let target: TerminalTarget`, used for `target.path` and as `addRunTab`'s `for:` argument.
- `store` — `@EnvironmentObject var store: AppStore`, called as `store.terminals.addRunTab(...)` (`AppStore.terminals` is `let terminals = TerminalSessions()`, `AppStore.swift:629` — the same type/likely-same-instance as `TerminalStatusBar.sessions`, just reached through `store` rather than a directly observed property).
- `agentManager` — dismiss the tab's banner.
- `agentPopoverTab` — the local `@State`, cleared to `nil` to close the popover.

Notably, the command run here is the **hardcoded literal `"claude"`**, not a seeded/quoted investigate command line — `AgentPrompt.investigateCommandLine(for:)` is never called in this closure, unlike the status-bar version.

## 3. Precise diff of the two popovers

Side by side, matching each corresponding piece:

**Attachment / presentation shape** — different:
- StatusBar: `.popover(isPresented: $showingDiagnosis, arrowEdge: .bottom) { TerminalAgentBanner(...) .frame(width: 380) }` directly chained on the diagnosis `Button`.
- TabStrip: `.popover(isPresented: Binding(get: { agentPopoverTab == tab.id }, set: { if !$0 { agentPopoverTab = nil } })) { agentPopover(for: tab) }` on the `TerminalTabChip`, with the actual `TerminalAgentBanner(...)` construction factored into a separate `@ViewBuilder` function that also does the `if let state = agentManager.banners[tab.id]` unwrap (StatusBar receives `bannerState` already unwrapped as a parameter from its caller).

**`TerminalAgentBanner(...)` call — argument by argument:**

- `state:` — StatusBar: `bannerState` (a passed-in `AgentBannerState`, non-optional param). TabStrip: `state` (locally unwrapped from `agentManager.banners[tab.id]`). Same shape, different source.

- `onDiagnose:` — nearly identical:
  - StatusBar: `{ agentManager.diagnose(tab: tabID, target: target.id) }`
  - TabStrip: `{ agentManager.diagnose(tab: tab.id, target: target.id) }`
  - Only difference: `tabID` (a stored property) vs `tab.id` (from the parameter). Otherwise byte-identical.

- `onInsertFix:` — differs in how the terminal's `view` is reached:
  - StatusBar: `{ fix in state?.view.sendText(fix); showingDiagnosis = false }` — uses the struct's `state: TerminalState?` directly.
  - TabStrip: `{ fix in if case .terminal(let s) = tab.content { s.view.sendText(fix) }; agentPopoverTab = nil }` — has to pattern-match `tab.content` to reach a `TerminalState` since it has no `state` property; also silently no-ops if the tab isn't a terminal (StatusBar's optional-chain does the same no-op, just via a different path).
  - Closing action: `showingDiagnosis = false` vs `agentPopoverTab = nil` — same intent (close this popover), different `@State` variable and different "closed" value (Bool `false` vs optional `nil`).

- `onInvestigate:` — most divergent block:
  - **cwd derivation**: StatusBar computes it in one line: `let cwd = state?.view.lastKnownCwd ?? target.path`. TabStrip needs an if/else because it must first determine if `tab.content` is a terminal at all:
    ```swift
    let cwd: String
    if case .terminal(let s) = tab.content {
      cwd = s.view.lastKnownCwd ?? target.path
    } else {
      cwd = target.path
    }
    ```
    Same fallback value (`target.path`) and same primary source (`.lastKnownCwd`), different control flow because of what's in scope.
  - **command construction**: StatusBar builds a seeded command via `AgentPrompt.investigateCommandLine(for: bannerState)` and a comment explaining why (`// Seed the interactive agent with the problem so it can start investigating immediately.`). TabStrip uses the bare string literal `"claude"` — it does not call `AgentPrompt` at all, and the diagnosis being unwrapped as `state` earlier is never fed into a seed prompt. This is a real behavioral difference, not just a style one.
  - **invoking `addRunTab`**: StatusBar: `_ = sessions.addRunTab(for: target, command: command, cwd: cwd)` using the `@ObservedObject var sessions: TerminalSessions`. TabStrip: `_ = store.terminals.addRunTab(for: target, command: "claude", cwd: cwd)` using `@EnvironmentObject var store: AppStore` → `.terminals`. Both ultimately call the same `TerminalSessions.addRunTab` method, just reached via a different reference (`sessions` vs `store.terminals`), and with a different `command:` argument.
  - **dismiss + close**: StatusBar: `agentManager.dismiss(tab: tabID); showingDiagnosis = false`. TabStrip: `agentManager.dismiss(tab: tab.id); agentPopoverTab = nil`. Structurally identical modulo the id source and close-state variable, same as `onDiagnose`/`onDismiss`.

- `onDismiss:` — identical shape:
  - StatusBar: `{ agentManager.dismiss(tab: tabID); showingDiagnosis = false }`
  - TabStrip: `{ agentManager.dismiss(tab: tab.id); agentPopoverTab = nil }`
  - Same as above: only the id accessor and the close-state variable/value differ.

**`.frame(width:)`** — different constants: StatusBar `380`, TabStrip `360`. No comment explains the discrepancy in either file.

**Summary of duplication:** The four closures (`onDiagnose`, `onInsertFix`, `onInvestigate`, `onDismiss`) are structurally identical between the two sites — same sequence of operations, same call shapes — but every single line differs in its concrete tokens because the two views hold different state shapes to get to the same underlying values (`TerminalState`, `TerminalTab.ID`, `TerminalSessions`, and a "close me" state var of different type). The one place they diverge in *behavior* rather than just plumbing is `onInvestigate`'s command construction (`AgentPrompt.investigateCommandLine(for:)` vs. the literal `"claude"`). The `TerminalAgentBanner(...)` call itself (five named arguments, same shapes) and the surrounding popover scaffolding are otherwise the same idea implemented twice, with a stray width-constant mismatch (380 vs 360).

## 4. `AgentPrompt` — full file

File: `/Users/joelmoss/dev/workroom/macapp/WorkroomApp/Core/AgentPrompt.swift` (144 lines total, quoted in full above in the read output). Key excerpts:

```swift
enum AgentPrompt {
  static let systemPrompt = """
    You are a terminal assistant inside a developer tool. ...
    """

  static let outputBegin = "----- BEGIN TERMINAL OUTPUT (untrusted data) -----"
  static let outputEnd = "----- END TERMINAL OUTPUT -----"

  static func userMessage(
    command: String?, cwd: String?, exitCode: Int32?, shell: String?, output: String
  ) -> String { ... }

  /// A shell command line that opens an interactive `claude` seeded with the failure so the agent can
  /// start investigating immediately (issue #49). Run in the pane's real cwd (unlike the neutral-dir
  /// no-tools diagnosis), so it can inspect the project.
  static func investigateCommandLine(for state: AgentBannerState) -> String {
    let (failure, diagnosis) = state.failureAndDiagnosis
    let prompt = investigatePrompt(
      command: failure.command, exitCode: failure.exitCode, diagnosis: diagnosis)
    return "claude " + shellSingleQuoted(prompt)
  }

  /// The natural-language seed prompt for the interactive Investigate session: the failed command,
  /// its exit code, and (when available) the quick diagnosis, then an instruction to dig in. Output
  /// is deliberately omitted — the agent runs in the real cwd and can re-run the command itself.
  static func investigatePrompt(command: String?, exitCode: Int32, diagnosis: AgentDiagnosis?)
    -> String
  {
    var lines: [String] = []
    if let command = nonBlank(command) {
      lines.append("I ran `\(command)` in this directory and it failed with exit code \(exitCode).")
    } else {
      lines.append("A command in this directory failed with exit code \(exitCode).")
    }
    if let diagnosis {
      lines.append("A quick automated check suggested this cause: \(diagnosis.summary)")
      if let fix = nonBlank(diagnosis.fixCommand) {
        lines.append("and this unverified fix: \(fix)")
      }
    }
    lines.append("Please investigate the root cause and help me fix it.")
    return lines.joined(separator: "\n")
  }

  /// POSIX single-quote a string for safe embedding as one shell argument — wrap in single quotes and
  /// escape any embedded single quote as `'\''`. Handles newlines/quotes/`$` in the seeded prompt.
  static func shellSingleQuoted(_ s: String) -> String {
    "'" + s.replacingOccurrences(of: "'", with: "'\\''") + "'"
  }

  static func parse(envelopeJSON: String) -> AgentDiagnosis? { ... }
  static func parseInner(_ text: String) -> AgentDiagnosis { ... }
  static func stripCodeFence(_ text: String) -> String { ... }
  private static func nonBlank(_ s: String?) -> String? { ... }
  private static func nonNullString(_ value: Any?) -> String? { ... }
}
```

Related type in the same file:
```swift
struct AgentDiagnosis: Equatable, Sendable {
  let summary: String
  let fixCommand: String?
  let detail: String?
}
```

## 5. `AgentBannerState`

Defined in `/Users/joelmoss/dev/workroom/macapp/WorkroomApp/Core/TerminalAgentManager.swift:29-53`:

```swift
/// What the banner shows for a tab (issue #49). Absent (no entry) means no banner.
enum AgentBannerState: Equatable, Sendable {
  /// An eligible failure that hasn't been diagnosed yet — shows a "Diagnose" button.
  case awaitingDiagnose(FailedCommand)
  /// A diagnosis is running.
  case loading(FailedCommand)
  /// A diagnosis came back.
  case ready(FailedCommand, AgentDiagnosis)
  /// The run failed (CLI missing, auth, timeout, …).
  case failure(FailedCommand, AgentErrorKind)
  /// A remote/ssh/tmux session — the local agent can't see the real host, so we caveat and disable
  /// Investigate rather than give a confidently-wrong local diagnosis (X5).
  case remoteCaveat(FailedCommand)

  /// The captured failure every state carries, plus the diagnosis when one is ready — for building
  /// the interactive Investigate seed prompt (issue #49).
  var failureAndDiagnosis: (FailedCommand, AgentDiagnosis?) {
    switch self {
    case .awaitingDiagnose(let failure), .loading(let failure), .failure(let failure, _),
      .remoteCaveat(let failure):
      return (failure, nil)
    case .ready(let failure, let diagnosis):
      return (failure, diagnosis)
    }
  }
}
```

It is produced/published by `TerminalAgentManager`:
```swift
@Published private(set) var banners: [TerminalTab.ID: AgentBannerState] = [:]
...
private func setBanner(_ state: AgentBannerState?, tab: TerminalTab.ID) {
  banners[tab] = state
}
```
— set from `commandFinished` (`.awaitingDiagnose`, `.remoteCaveat`), `start` (`.loading`), and `apply(outcome:tab:failure:)` (`.ready`/`.failure`) (`TerminalAgentManager.swift:134-243`).

Each call site's instance:
- **`TerminalStatusBar`**: `private var diagnosis: AgentBannerState? { state == nil ? nil : agentManager.banners[tabID] }` (line 38), then `if let diagnosis { diagnosisSegment(diagnosis) }` (line 59) passes it into `diagnosisSegment(_ bannerState: AgentBannerState)`, which is where `onInvestigate` closes over it as `bannerState`.
- **`TerminalTabStrip`**: looked up inline inside `agentPopover(for:)`: `if let state = agentManager.banners[tab.id] { TerminalAgentBanner(state: state, ...) }` (lines 429-430) — same `agentManager.banners` dictionary, keyed the same way, but resolved fresh in this function rather than threaded in as a parameter, and there's no equivalent of `TerminalStatusBar`'s `diagnosis` computed property gating on a non-nil `TerminalState?` first.

## 6. `TerminalSessions.addRunTab`

File: `/Users/joelmoss/dev/workroom/macapp/WorkroomApp/Core/TerminalSessions.swift:972-987`.

The doc comment that applies to it is the block starting at line 945 (it documents the whole "run command" tab feature, including the ASCII lifecycle diagram spanning lines 945-970, ending just above `addRunTab`'s `@discardableResult`):

```swift
  /// Open the dedicated "run command" terminal (issue #7): a solo tab that launches `command` in
  /// `cwd`, titled "Run" until the program reports its own title, focused like any new tab — through
  /// `setFocused`, so focus observers fire (it is NOT a direct dict write; see the focus-chokepoint
  /// note on `setFocused`). The caller (`AppStore`) owns the run-state and wires `onChildExited`;
  /// this just creates and shows the tab.
  ///
  /// Run-tab lifecycle — one `AppStore.RunState` per target; the pane stays open on exit via
  /// `wait_after_command`:
  /// ```
  ///   armed                       auto-run queued before the workroom's pane exists; consumed on mount
  ///   start (no state / armed) ─▶ spawn surface (command = $SHELL -lic '<cmd>', wait_after_command);
  ///                               focus; state = running
  ///
  ///   running ──────── Run / ⌘R ─────────▶ focus (no respawn)
  ///   running ──────── Stop (1st) ───────▶ Ctrl-C; state = running(interrupted)
  ///   running ──────── Restart ──────────▶ Ctrl-C; state = restarting
  ///   running / running(interrupted) ── child exits ─▶ stopped (pane open)
  ///   running(interrupted) ── Stop (2nd) ─▶ closeTab → ghostty_surface_free (SIGHUP, hard kill)
  ///   restarting ── child exits ─▶ close + respawn (graceful; frees the port); Stop ─▶ running(interrupted)
  ///   stopped ──────── Run / ⌘R / Restart ─▶ close + respawn
  ///   any state ────── close tab (⌘W/✕) / reap ─▶ removed (state cleared via onTabsRemoved)
  /// ```
  /// A backgrounded run tab is never the active tab, so it never mounts — and a libghostty surface
  /// spawns its process only on window-mount. We give the off-window `ensureSurfaceCreated` a sane
  /// initial size so the command starts with reasonable COLUMNS/LINES; it re-sizes on first real mount.
  static let backgroundRunInitialSize = CGSize(width: 800, height: 480)

  @discardableResult
  func addRunTab(for target: TerminalTarget, command: String, cwd: String, focus: Bool = true)
    -> TerminalTab
  {
    let tab = makeRunTab(for: target, command: command, cwd: cwd)
    insert(tab, for: target)
    if focus {
      setFocused(tab.id, for: target.id)
    } else {
      // Issue #67 (run in the background): not focusing means the pane never mounts, so spawn the
      // surface off-window now — otherwise the command would never start and the toast would lie.
      tab.surface?.ensureSurfaceCreated(initialSize: Self.backgroundRunInitialSize)
    }
    reconcileOcclusion(for: target)
    return tab
  }
```

Note there's a static stored property (`backgroundRunInitialSize`) sandwiched between the doc comment and `addRunTab` itself — the doc comment isn't immediately-adjacent to the function signature in the source, but its content (the run-tab lifecycle) is clearly about `addRunTab`/`respawnRunTab`/`makeRunTab`, the three functions that follow it.

