## 6. Teardown path: `onChildExited`, SIGHUP-on-free, graceful-close helper

**`markRunExited`** (the `onChildExited` handler) — `AppStore.swift:2209-2242`:
```swift
  /// The surface's PTY child exited — under the supervisor model that's the SUPERVISOR itself, so the
  /// whole run is over (a quit/teardown, or a supervisor crash), not a user-command stop (those are
  /// driven by the status file). Mark terminal; the pane stays open (`wait_after_command`). If the
  /// status file already recorded an outcome (the usual case — the supervisor wrote `exited <code>`
  /// before exiting), keep it; otherwise fall back to libghostty's code. The forked-free backstop
  /// (SIGKILL the captured session) guards against a leaf that outlived the supervisor.
  private func markRunExited(for target: TerminalTarget, exitCode: UInt32) {
    guard let tab = runStates[target.id]?.tab else { return }
    let sid = runSessions[target.id] ?? -1
    if runOutcomes[target.id] == nil, let ctl = runPidFiles[target.id],
      let raw = try? String(
        contentsOfFile: Self.supervisorStatusPath(forPidPath: ctl), encoding: .utf8)
    {
      let line = raw.trimmingCharacters(in: .whitespacesAndNewlines)
      if !line.isEmpty { applyRunStatus(line, for: target) }
    }
    runStates[target.id] = .stopped(tab: tab)
    if runOutcomes[target.id] == nil {
      runOutcomes[target.id] = .exited(code: Int32(truncatingIfNeeded: exitCode))
      scheduleRunToastAutoDismiss(for: target.id)
      if Self.runOutcomeIsBannerWorthy(runOutcomes[target.id]) {
        postRunFailureBannerIfBackgrounded(
          targetID: target.id, tabID: tab, title: "Run failed",
          body: "exited with code \(exitCode)")
      }
    }
    clearRunPidFile(for: target.id)
    waitForSessionsExit(sid > 1 ? [sid] : [], deadline: Date().addingTimeInterval(6)) {}
  }
```

**SIGHUP-on-free behavior** — documented at `GhosttySurfaceView.swift:738-746`:
```swift
  /// Whether a child process is still running in this surface — vs. already exited with the pane held
  /// open by `wait_after_command`, or no surface yet. The run-command graceful stop (issue #7) keys
  /// off this: only a LIVE process needs a Ctrl-C + wait-for-exit before its surface is freed, so that
  /// a dev server (e.g. Puma) cleans up its pidfile/port instead of being orphaned by a bare
  /// surface-free (a PTY hangup → SIGHUP, which Puma ignores → "A server is already running" later).
  var hasLiveProcess: Bool {
    if let liveProcessOverrideForTesting { return liveProcessOverrideForTesting }
    return surface != nil && !processHasExited
  }
```
and `destroySurface()` (`GhosttySurfaceView.swift:319-324`): `if let surface { ghostty_surface_free(surface) }` — the raw free that triggers the PTY hangup.

**The graceful-close helper**, `closingRunTab` — `AppStore.swift:2333-2347`:
```swift
  /// If `tabID` is the target's run command with a live process, Ctrl-C it and wait for it to exit
  /// before running `proceed` (the actual close/teardown); otherwise run `proceed` now. Ordinary
  /// shell tabs (which don't exit on Ctrl-C) and already-exited run tabs fall straight through, so
  /// only a live dev server pays the wait.
  private func closingRunTab(
    _ tabID: TerminalTab.ID, for targetID: TerminalTarget.ID, then proceed: @escaping () -> Void
  ) {
    let isRunTab = runStates[targetID]?.tab == tabID
    let live = liveRunView(for: targetID) != nil
    guard isRunTab, live else {
      proceed()  // not a live run tab → free directly (no supervisor to gracefully stop)
      return
    }
    gracefullyStopRuns([targetID], then: proceed)
  }
```
and `gracefullyStopRuns` / `pollUntilExited` (`AppStore.swift:2261-2308`) which sends `SIGTERM` to the supervisor per target, polls `hasLiveProcess`, and SIGKILLs the captured session (`runSessions`) as a backstop on timeout — never frees the surface itself.

Note `TerminalSessions.makeRunTab` (`TerminalSessions.swift:1033-1047`) sets a **default, non-graceful** `onCloseRequested` that just calls `closeTab` directly:
```swift
  private func makeRunTab(for target: TerminalTarget, command: String, cwd: String) -> TerminalTab {
    let tab = makeTerminalTab(for: target, cwd: cwd, command: command, title: "Run")
    let targetID = target.id
    let tabID = tab.id
    tab.surface?.onCloseRequested = { [weak self] in
      guard let self, let target = self.target(forID: targetID) else { return }
      self.closeTab(tabID, for: target)
    }
    return tab
  }
```
`AppStore.wireRunClose` (§1) **overwrites** this immediately after `addRunTab`/`respawnRunTab` return, replacing it with the `closingRunTab`-gated version.

## 7. Existing tests

`macapp/WorkroomAppTests/RunCommandTests.swift` (1249 lines, 67 test funcs) covers `runStates`/`isRunCommandRunning`/`runTabID`/`hasLiveRunCommand` extensively, e.g.:
- `testReapClearsRunState` (561), `testClosingRunTabClearsState` (408), `testClosingTabMidRestartDoesNotRespawnTheNextRun` (576)
- `testSecondStopWaitsForLiveProcessThenCloses` (701), `testRequestCloseWaitsForLiveRunCommand` (725)
- `testGracefullyStopAllWaitsForEveryLiveCommandThenCompletes` (742), `testGracefullyStopAllCompletesImmediatelyWhenNothingLive` (774), `testGracefullyStopAllFallsBackAfterTimeout` (785) — these directly exercise `store.hasLiveRunCommand` and `store.gracefullyStopAllRunCommands`
- `testRunOrFocusForTargetChecksOwnerForThePassedTarget` (333) — exercises `WindowRegistry.runOwner` via two registered windows/stores, with the comment: *"the branch reads `runStates[target.id]`, so a regression that reverted i..."* (line 331)

`macapp/WorkroomAppTests/MultiWindowTests.swift`:
- `testCloseGuardAllowsCloseWithoutRunCommand` (143) — the only test touching `WindowCloseGuard`/`hasLiveRunCommand` at the window-close-guard level.

**No test directly exercises `AppStore.applicationShouldTerminate` / `stopRunCommandsThenTerminate` / `WindowRegistry.gracefullyStopAllWindows`** — those are only referenced in comments (`SessionSaveTests.swift:235`, `ScrollbackSidecarTests.swift:232`). The quit-time behavior is verified one layer down, via `hasLiveRunCommand` / `gracefullyStopAllRunCommands` (store level) and `WindowCloseGuard` (per-window level), not the `NSApplicationDelegate` entry point itself.

## Key question: every consumer of `runStates` today

Enumerated from the grep above, grouped by what they'd need to also consult against a second (agent-tab) ownership record:

1. **`AppStore.isRunCommandRunning(for:)`** (1546) — toolbar/sidebar/menu Run↔Stop toggle source of truth.
2. **`AppStore.runTabID(for:)`** (1551) — "which tab is the run tab" for status bar / tab-strip chip / toast open.
3. **`AppStore.hasAnyRunTerminal`** (2159) — Go ▸ Run Terminal menu gate.
4. **`AppStore.revealRunTerminal()`** (2164-2174) — Go ▸ Run Terminal navigation, picks any target with a run tab.
5. **`AppStore.runFailed(for:)`** via `runOutcomes` (paired with `runStates`) — red-icon state, read by `WorkroomTabBar` and `TerminalTabStrip`.
6. **`AppStore.runOrFocusRunCommand(for:)`** (2095-2140) — the ⌘R state machine (`switch runStates[target.id]`).
7. **`AppStore.stopRunCommand(for:)`** (2181-2189) and **`restartRunCommand(for:)`** (2196-2207) — ⇧⌘R/⌥⌘R state machines.
8. **`AppStore.toggleRunCommand(for:)`** (2025-2033) — sidebar row toggle, delegates into #6/#7.
9. **`AppStore.applyRunStatus(_:for:)`** (1846-1882) — the status-file poller's state-transition function.
10. **`AppStore.markRunExited(for:exitCode:)`** (2215-2242) — the `onChildExited` handler.
11. **`AppStore.liveRunView(for:)`** / **`hasLiveRunCommand`** (2248-2259) — quit/close teardown gate.
12. **`AppStore.closingRunTab(_:for:then:)`** (2337-2347) — per-tab close gate (used by `wireRunClose` and the default `TerminalSessions` close path via `requestCloseTerminalTab`).
13. **`AppStore.gracefullyStopAllRunCommands`** (2352-2354) — quit-time bulk stop, iterates `runStates.keys`.
14. **`AppStore.armAutoRun` / `ensureInitialTerminal`** (2358, 2376-2393) — the `.armed` pre-tab-mount case.
15. **`AppStore.onTabsRemoved` handler** (916-931) — clears `runStates[targetID]` when the run tab closes/reaps.
16. **`AppStore.reapTargetLocally`** (3079-3089) — clears `runStates[targetID]` on workroom delete.
17. **create-workroom failure path** (2907) — disarms a `.armed` entry on setup failure.
18. **`AppStore.runToastItems`** (1441-1480) and **`openRunToast(for:)`** (1491-1494) — the run-status toast surface.
19. **`WindowRegistry.runOwner(for:excluding:)`** (WindowRegistry.swift:210-212) — single-owner-across-windows check, reads `$0.runStates[target]?.isRunning`.
20. **`TerminalStatusBar.runStatePresentation`** (TerminalStatusBar.swift:223-244) — the only *view* reading `store.runStates` directly (not through a helper).

Everything else in the UI layer (`TargetDetailToolbar.RunControls`, `ProjectSidebar.RowRunButton`, `WorkroomTabBar`, `TerminalTabStrip`, `RootView`'s menu wiring, `SwitcherRailView`) goes through #1/#2/#5 rather than the dict directly, but all ultimately key off the same one-entry-per-`TerminalTarget.ID` assumption baked into `RunState.tab`/`isRunning`. A second ownership record for interactive agent tabs would need to be consulted anywhere `runStates[id]?.tab` or `.isRunning` is used to answer "is *a* process running/owned for this target" if an agent tab should also count — that's essentially the full list above, since `runStates` is the single source of truth the toolbar, sidebar, tab strip, status bar, quit teardown, and cross-window ownership all read from one `@Published` dict.

